<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pax Britannica</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pax Britannica refers to the period of relative peace among the great powers from 1815 to the early twentieth century, during which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was the leading global power. It rested on the dominance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, maintained on the principle of being equal to any two foreign navies combined. This naval supremacy allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> to protect its trade routes and project force worldwide.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pax-britannica.jpg" alt="Pax Britannica" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arthur Mees · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Mees_Flags_of_A_Free_Empire_1910_Cornell_CUL_PJM_1167_01_(Reddit_source).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Pax Britannica, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for British Peace and modelled on the Pax Romana, refers to the relative peace between the great powers in the period roughly bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. During it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> became the world hegemon, developed an informal empire and took the role of a global policeman. Between 1815 and 1914 about 26,000,000 square kilometres of territory and roughly 400 million people were added to the empire. Victory over Napoleonic France left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> without any serious rival, other than perhaps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Free trade became a central principle of British practice by the 1840s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> controlled most key maritime trade routes, and British merchants, shippers and bankers had such an advantage that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> controlled trade with countries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, Siam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> outside its formal empire. A British peace on the trade routes began in 1815 with the annexation of British Ceylon. Britain imposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-maritime-treaty-of-1820">General Maritime Treaty of 1820</a> on the Arab rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, who in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perpetual-maritime-truce">Perpetual Maritime Truce</a> of 1853 gave up the right to wage war at sea in return for British protection.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> suppressed piracy and blocked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, setting up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa-squadron">West Africa Squadron</a> to enforce the ban of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abolition-of-the-atlantic-slave-trade">Slave Trade Act</a> 1807. It was superior to any other two navies combined. Sea power did not project on land, however, and the major powers fought land wars, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. The Royal Navy also fought the First and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-opium-war">Second Opium Wars</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Anglo-Egyptian War led to a British occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> lasting seven decades. The historian A. J. P. Taylor called it a great event, the only real event in international relations between the Battle of Sedan and the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the Russo-Japanese war. By 1902 the empire was linked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> network known as the All Red Line.</p><p>The Pax Britannica weakened as the continental order established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> broke down, with the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, the emergence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, and the industrialisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1914 marked its end. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> remained one of the leading powers until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pax Britannica” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This gave Britain the capacity to protect its trade routes, project force along the world's coastlines and underpin the <b>Pax Britannica</b> through credible deterrence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>British writers spoke of a <b>Pax Britannica</b> that ended local wars, and Frederick Lugard’s “dual mandate” held that colonial rule should develop Africa’s resources for the world while advancing the welfare of its peoples.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>…American colonies in 1776; it turned to India, Australia, Asia, Africa and the Pacific, and after 1815 enjoyed a century of almost unchallenged dominance, the <b>Pax Britannica</b>, which was still an almost uninterrupted series of colonial wars, from the Anglo-Burmese and Opium Wars to the Scramble for Africa.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/imperialism">Imperialism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
